{
  "packs": [
    {
      "id": "basics",
      "version": "60902.6"
    },
    {
      "id": "barriers",
      "version": "60823.1",
      "via": [
        "basics"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "git-github",
      "version": "60902.1",
      "via": [
        "basics"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "html",
      "version": "60902.1"
    },
    {
      "id": "tidy-repo",
      "version": "60902.2"
    },
    {
      "id": "claudinite-growth",
      "version": "60902.6",
      "config": {
        "retention_days": 10
      }
    },
    "local/edfringe",
    "local/edfringe-data",
    "local/edfringe-requirements",
    {
      "id": "executable-requirements",
      "version": "60902.1",
      "config": {
        "spec": "product/requirements.md"
      },
      "answers": {
        "ui_testing": "Headless Chromium via Playwright (pre-installed in the environment): render the real single-page app — Leaflet map included, with map tiles and other network faked deterministically — and assert pixel-exact visual goldens the owner reviews by sight; short per-leaf tests drive to a state and capture. Pure planner rules (plan/lib) stay node --test logic leaves. No visual harness exists yet — this is the target mechanism.",
        "requirements_source": "Authored fresh at product/requirements.md (config.spec), distilled from the existing product brief docs/product-spec.md. No prior formal requirements doc or issue-tracker backlog to extract."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "spec-driven-product",
      "version": "60902.1"
    },
    {
      "id": "static-website",
      "version": "60902.1",
      "answers": {
        "hosting": "GitHub Pages from this repo — confirmed from the repo rather than asked: the hand-rolled Pages workflow this adoption replaces already deployed to Pages on push to main via actions/deploy-pages, with no CNAME and no other host. So the full set is vendored: orchestrator + publish/deploy reusables + the three composite actions.",
        "publish_set": "Rooted at the repo root. The site is the four pages (index, accessibility, privacy, terms), the favicons and .nojekyll, css/, js/, shared/, the planner's own paths (plan/index.html, plan/plan.css, plan/plan.js, plan/lib), package.json (the debug pill fetches it for the version), and only the data the browser actually fetches: data/venues.json, data/days/, and the three normalized sidecars the planner loads (shows.min.json, availability.min.json, descriptions.min.json). Derived from the fetch call sites in js/app.js and plan/plan.js, not from 'everything except the tooling'. Deliberately excluded: data/normalized/shows.json (7.4M) and data/prices.json (2.9M) are scraper-side inputs no page fetches, plus scraper/, docs/, design-concepts/, product-wiki/, plan/design/, scripts/ and the agent tooling."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "product-wiki",
      "version": "60902.2",
      "answers": {
        "product": "Fringe Discover: a spontaneity/reachability tool for the Edinburgh Festival Fringe. Where listings apps answer 'what exists?', this answers 'what can I actually make right now?' — given the user's location, how far they'll travel and how, and their next commitment, it collapses ~3,800 shows into a short list of genuinely reachable options that haven't started and still leave time to reach whatever is next.",
        "users": "Fringe-goers mid-festival, usually on foot and without a fixed plan — visitors with a gap between a booked show and dinner, locals and tourists intimidated by the programme's scale. Decisive in the moment rather than researchers: they want a short list of good, reachable options, not 3,800 search results. Emotional target: 'delight + relief'.",
        "market": "Edinburgh Fringe discovery and planning tools. Alternatives: the official Fringe app (a 'Nearby now' + shake-to-search finder), Plan My Fringe (a schedule optimiser with a walking-time 'Fringe Trail'), the edfringe.com programme and filters, the Half Price Hut (same-day discounts), review outlets such as ThreeWeeks, plus street posters and word-of-mouth. None centres full reachability — travel mode plus next-commitment slack — the way Fringe Discover does."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "claude-code-web-users-support",
      "version": "60902.1",
      "config": {
        "repo": "missingbulb/Shepherd"
      },
      "answers": {
        "store": "missingbulb/Shepherd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "claudinite-lifecycle",
      "version": "60902.3"
    },
    {
      "id": "claudinite-tasks",
      "version": "60902.9"
    }
  ],
  "accept": [
    {
      "rule": "file-placement",
      "path": "product/requirements/",
      "reason": "the executable-requirements layout is a framework contract: cases live in <kind>/cases/ and the harness in shared/, so every case's reach to shared/ and to the shipped modules under js/, plan/ and shared/ is fixed by the pack's layout standard, not a placement choice"
    },
    {
      "rule": "claudinite-isolation",
      "path": "scripts/verify.sh",
      "reason": "the repo's one verification gate invokes the world sweep (engine/checks/check_the_world.mjs) so that passing it locally means what passing CI means — a commit that passed verify and went red on CI for a blocking conformance finding is what prompted this. Not the coupling the rule guards against: no canon internals are imported and nothing is inlined, it is the same documented CLI entry point .github/workflows/ already spells under the rule's own carve-out. A canon rename of that one path breaks the workflow and this script identically, and the fix is the same one line"
    }
  ],
  "taskScheduler": {
    "disabledTasks": [
      "edfringe/refresh-shows",
      "edfringe/refresh-tickets"
    ],
    "agenticTaskInvocationEndpoints": {
      "default": {
        "url": "[REDACTED:env:ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL]/v1/claude_code/routines/trig_019ysxedonGvJiDzVZbJamBd/fire",
        "tokenSecret": "CCR_ROUTINE_TOKEN"
      }
    },
    "dailyHour": 4,
    "weeklyDay": "Sun",
    "monthlyDay": 1
  },
  "engineVersion": "60902.1"
}

// A member's own task declarations become data (#1633): every
// `.claudinite/local/packs/<pack>/tasks/<name>/task.mjs` is converted to a
// `task.json` pointing at the task schema, and the module is deleted.
//
// THE WRITE IS THE ENGINE'S. `taskDeclarationsToJson: true` names the registry's
// named codemod (engine/migrations/registry.mjs, `applyTaskDeclarationConversion`),
// which runs the same converter the CLI does. A record cannot carry it: the JSON is
// the module's evaluated export, which no textual rewrite produces.
//
// GATED ON THE MOUNT, BY CONTENT. Converting is only safe once the member's
// vendored `claudinite-tasks` reads `task.json` — this pack's discover goes
// through `task-declaration.mjs` from the version this record lands with, and an
// older mount would see a folder with no `task.mjs` and silently run nothing.
// The two lanes deliver on their own cadences, so `appliesTo` probes the mounted
// pack for that module rather than trusting the stamp; an unreadable mount reads
// as "not capable" and the record stays inert. The canon runs the same probe
// against its own tree (two-root form).
//
// IDEMPOTENT by construction: a folder with no `task.mjs` is nothing to convert.
//
// THE APPLY STAGE WRITES THE DESCRIPTIONS. A converted declaration has every field
// its module had and no `description`, because a description is judgment — what
// the task does, or why it exists, in the words a reader of the pack wants — and
// the code cannot write one. The module's comments went into the task's README
// (the converter's doing), so the session has the author's own words to work from.
// The stage runs once per member, on the update that delivers this record; on a
// member with no local tasks it finds nothing to write and ends.
const READER = 'packs/claudinite-tasks/task-declaration-text.mjs';
const mountReadsJson = async (read) => {
  const text = (await read(`.claudinite/shared/${READER}`)) ?? (await read(READER));
  return Boolean(text) && text.includes("'task.json'");
};

export default {
  id: 'task-declarations-json',
  landed: '2026-09-02',
  version: '60903.3',
  summary: 'a member\'s local-pack task.mjs declarations are converted to task.json, the data form the engine now reads (#1633)',

  appliesTo: mountReadsJson,
  taskDeclarationsToJson: true,

  applyStage: {
    why: 'each converted local-pack task declaration needs a description only a reader of the task can write',
    instructions: [
      'The deterministic half converted every `.claudinite/local/packs/<pack>/tasks/<name>/task.mjs`',
      'in this repo to a `task.json`, moving the module\'s comments into that task\'s `README.md`',
      'under "Why the declaration reads as it does". What it could not write is the',
      '`description` field. For every `task.json` under `.claudinite/local/packs/` that has',
      'no `description`, add one: up to fifty words on what the task does, or why it exists,',
      'read from the task\'s README, its `task.md` if it has one, and its worker. It must not',
      'restate what the other fields already say — never the cadence, the conditions, the',
      'automerge policy, or where the files live. Place it right after `id`, keep the file\'s',
      'other keys and its indentation as they are, and do not edit the vendored mount under',
      '`.claudinite/shared/`. A task.json that already carries a description is left alone.',
      'If no local task.json lacks one, there is nothing to do here.',
    ].join('\n'),
  },

  // The telemetry hook cannot list directories, and the fact this record retires
  // — a `task.mjs` under the member's local packs — is only visible by listing.
  // The fleet-visible signal is the shape check's advisory on every remaining
  // module (task-declaration-shape); the cleanup that drops the module reader is
  // gated on that advisory firing nowhere (#1633).
  legacyPresent: async () => false,
};

